{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-core":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-core/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/prose-intro-heisenberg-commutator","@type":["folio-assistant-core:Prose","doco:Section"],"label":"prose:intro-heisenberg-commutator","kind":"prose","text":"intro-heisenberg-commutator.md","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"prose:intro-heisenberg-commutator\"></a><p>The Heisenberg commutator <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">[\\hat{p}, \\hat{q}] = -i\\hbar</code> emerges from the fibre structure of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathcal{S}</code> rather than as a postulated relation. This section proves the substrate Heisenberg commutator as a structural identity and exhibit the non-commutative drag that the categorical commutator imparts on the geometry.</p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/quantum-observable-universes/intro-heisenberg-commutator.md","heading":"","page":"b/prose-intro-heisenberg-commutator","qa":{"counts":{"pass":14,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":5,"unknown":0,"stale":18},"milnor":{"total":1,"notPassing":1}}}
